---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Question from '../components/Question.astro';
import AngelOnMission from '../components/AngelOnMission.astro';
const pageTitle = "Angelic Infrastructure";
---
<Layout pageTitle={pageTitle}>
    <AngelOnMission alt="The Angelic Warrior, in flight" />
    <h1>Angelic Infrastructure</h1>
    <span class="badge">Opinionated Infrastructure (Developer)</span>

    <div class="prose">

    <h2>We build the tools for you to use the best proven technologies.</h2>

    <p>
        If you want rapid iteration, want to repatriate your data, or
        hate yaml — we can help.
    </p>

    <h3>The AngelBox Capability</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>Model Generated Reproducible Infrastructure - Zero Drift</li>
        <li>Rapid config change turnaround - Updates land in &lt; 1 min.</li>
        <li>Develop (virtually) in production - Instant Deployment, rapid feedback loop.</li>
        <li>Instant Backups - Snapshots, as safe as possible.</li>
        <li>Portable Infrastructure and Data - Bring your cloud back on-prem.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Supported Products</h3>

    <p><strong>Data Sovereignty Solutions - Server Appliances</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>AMI NixOS - Root on ZFS</li>
        <li>AMI Internal DNS/Certificate Authority</li>
        <li>NextCloud - Freedom from Microsoft</li>
        <li>ForgeGo - Freedom from GitHub</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Server Farms</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Dashboard managed website-farm</li>
        <li>GoCD per team - real pipelines (yaml free)</li>
        <li>Any service as a scalable server farm.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Services</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>PHP Hosting Fixer-upper - Migrate to self-updating server farm</li>
        <li>PHP Site - Fixer-upper - Migrate to latest PHP versions</li>
        <li>Beyond PHP - PHP to Elixir-Phoenix migration</li>
    </ul>

    </div>

    <Question
        number={1}
        question="The three sub-missions"
        hint="AngelBox, PHP Rescue Services, Cloud Repatriation. The Missions page expands on each. What is the one-sentence outcome of each — the thing that is different for the customer once it is done?"
    />

    <Question
        number={2}
        question="What is the one call to action?"
        hint="One URL. The persona is strict about this: exactly one CTA to one URL, no stacked CTAs. And it should be the outcome, not the mechanism — get in touch, not read the docs."
    />

    <Question
        number={3}
        question="What question do you leave the reader with?"
        hint="The closing line. The worked example ends on one: 'What would you expect of your infrastructure in order to call it angelic?' A question that makes them think about their own infrastructure is worth more than a call to action, and it can do both."
    />

    <Question
        number={4}
        question="The history, or no history?"
        hint="From aerospace to agile pioneer to meta-tools. The aerospace training, British Aerospace and the factory floor, the Portland Patterns Repository and 1,800 tests, Kent Beck, the Agile Manifesto. Is that on this page, or is it for the Medium about page instead? The persona says no padding with backstory — so decide deliberately."
    />

    <Question
        number={5}
        question="What is the meta-tool argument?"
        hint="Bash is the integration plane and it is seventies technology. Claude Code, Gemini Code, Pi-Agent all run on it. We need a developer's Excel — a programmable, inspectable, observable integration plane. Does it belong here as a thesis, or is it a separate article that links back?"
    />

    <Question
        number={6}
        question="Who is this for?"
        hint="The reader, specifically. Not 'dev teams' — a team that needs senior platform capability without committing to a full-time hire, and who is deciding whether a fractional engagement is worth it. What are they doing on the day they find this page, and what went wrong that made them look?"
    />

    <Question
        number={7}
        question="Why should anyone believe you?"
        hint="The proof, with numbers where there are numbers. Thirty years shipping the same way. AngelBox, Formae, and the tools they have shipped. If there is a customer story with a real number behind it, that is the strongest thing on this page — where is it, and what is the number?"
    />

    <Question
        number={8}
        question="What is deliberately not on this page?"
        hint="The persona says do not expose internal module names — AngelBox and Formae — unless the customer would name them. If this is the manifesto, then the products have their own page. Confirm that, and confirm the one email address or booking link is the only route to you."
    />

    <h2>Skills</h2>
    <div class="skills center">
        <img class="skills__img" width="46" height="46" alt="NixOS" src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/devicons/devicon@latest/icons/nixos/nixos-original.svg"/>
        <img class="skills__img" width="46" height="46" alt="Talos" src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/devicons/devicon@latest/icons/talos/talos-original.svg"/>
        <img class="skills__img" width="46" height="46" alt="Pkl" src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/devicons/devicon@latest/icons/pkl/pkl-original.svg"/>
        <img class="skills__img" width="46" height="46" alt="ZFS" src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/devicons/devicon@latest/icons/zfs/zfs-original.svg"/>
        <img class="skills__img" width="46" height="46" alt="Podman" src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/devicons/devicon@latest/icons/podman/podman-original.svg"/>
        <img class="skills__img" width="46" height="46" alt="Kubernetes" src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/devicons/devicon@latest/icons/kubernetes/kubernetes-original.svg"/>
        <img class="skills__img" width="46" height="46" alt="Git" src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/devicons/devicon@latest/icons/git/git-original.svg"/>
    </div>
    <h2>Interests</h2>
    <p>
        Infrastructure that remains legible, automatable, and owned by the
        people who operate it. Declarative foundations, immutable operating
        systems, typed configuration, and tooling that makes the dangerous path
        explicit rather than magical. Bring us the awkward part — the
        integration nobody enjoys, or the system still held together by
        folklore. That is where useful work begins.
    </p>
</Layout>
